createMuxUpload
    ↓
Mux
    ↓
videos/{videoId}
```

These must be consolidated without losing Mux functionality.

### 5.1 Create Post becomes the canonical publishing entry point

All video publications must use the Create Post publishing system.

Do not build a second independent Sparks composer.

Sparks can open Create Post with the video destination or publishing mode already selected.

The author should be able to publish one video publication and select the applicable distribution surfaces.

The publication must retain one identity regardless of how many eligible surfaces display it.

### 5.2 Preserve and consolidate Mux

Do not replace Mux with a simpler upload that loses adaptive streaming or video processing capabilities.

Retain and consolidate the genuinely useful existing infrastructure:

* Direct uploads.

* Mux asset creation.

* Upload authorization.

* Webhook verification.

* Asset-ready handling.

* Playback ID management.

* Adaptive streaming.

* Signed playback.

* Watermarking.

* Video moderation.

* Audio fingerprinting where applicable.

* Processing failure handling.

* Retry and cleanup.

* Download authorization.

Use one canonical video-media upload pipeline.

Images, audio and other media must continue to use their appropriate existing media pipeline.

Do not upload the same video independently to Firebase Storage and Mux unless a proven, necessary product requirement justifies both copies.

Prevent duplicate thumbnails and redundant media uploads.

### 5.3 Use the canonical post identity through the video lifecycle

The video upload and publication workflows must correlate their processing with the canonical post and its media asset.

Mux webhook events must resolve to the correct media asset and associated canonical publication.

The webhook must never depend on a second social publication document.

Use stable identifiers and metadata; never depend on a filename or client-generated value as proof of ownership.

Handle these lifecycle states using a consistent, server-authoritative transition model compatible with existing post contracts:

```text
DRAFT
  ↓
UPLOAD_RESERVED
  ↓
UPLOADING
  ↓
PROCESSING
  ↓
MODERATION
  ↓
READY_TO_PUBLISH
  ↓
PUBLISHED
```

Failure paths must support:

```text
UPLOAD_FAILED
PROCESSING_FAILED
MODERATION_REJECTED
PUBLICATION_FAILED
CANCELLED
```

Do not make partially uploaded, unprocessed, rejected or unauthorized videos publicly discoverable.

Account for the case where a Mux webhook arrives before the final publication operation. Make this race safe using the existing draft/upload reservation infrastructure or a minimal, justified extension.

All transitions must be idempotent.

### 5.4 Duration and publishing rules

Align duration validation across:

* Create Post UI.

* Video editor.

* Client validation.

* Upload service.

* Backend callable.

* Mux processing.

* Sparks feed eligibility.

* Video detail.

* Firestore rules where applicable.

For Sparks, preserve the previously established product requirement of supporting approximately 1-second to 10-minute videos.

The existing dedicated-video configuration contains a 90-second Reel limit, a three-second minimum and a separate longer-video limit.

Do not retain conflicting limits that prevent the intended Sparks experience.

Different publication formats may have different eligibility constraints, but each must have one authoritative validation contract enforced on both client and server.

Do not weaken file size, account-tier, daily upload or abuse protections.

